<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Here's a test for Jax? Why is a bigot a "bigot"?
<[Kevin] APPERT> CBW weapons drifting over from an Iraqui purge of some ethnic 
minority ( I did not specify water vapor clouds)
<[80jax196] JAX> sounds nasty in French.
<[gars] GARY-S> That's where the naval observatory is, so it has become the 
modern reference point for the new moon to intiate Ramadan
<[Kevin] APPERT> whose navels do they observe?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> It's from the Spanish word for moustache.  Now why does 
moustache mean bigot?
<[gars] GARY-S> orange ones
<[80jax196] JAX> No se'.
<[Kevin] APPERT> why, Wil?
<[gars] GARY-S> The clouds you speak of should follow the new moon by roughly 2 
nights
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> The word originally meant Visigoth, who were known for their 
moustaches and Puritanical Unitarianism.
<[80jax196] JAX> pico, -are (L.) to smear with pitch.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> They were so obnoxious there was a crusade to eliminate them.
<[80jax196] JAX> probably part of the reason.
<[Kevin] APPERT> finaly some mention of Visigoths!   I've been wondering why 
those darn Vandals get all the good press.
<[80jax196] JAX> Slang words rarely have one sole deriviation.
<[80jax196] JAX> They are usually fortuitous intersections of several 
linguistic coincidences.
